WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.134 --> 00:00:03.135 line:0%
And finally, new rule:
since so much of what passes

00:00:03.235 --> 00:00:05.872 line:0%
for today's journalism is
anything but,

00:00:06.239 --> 00:00:08.908 line:0%
how about some rules for
identifying actual news?

00:00:09.408 --> 00:00:11.444 line:0%
For example, when an internet
headline reads:

00:00:11.544 --> 00:00:12.445 line:0%
"You won't believe..."

00:00:12.545 --> 00:00:14.547 line:0%
Yes, you will,
and no, it's not news.

00:00:14.747 --> 00:00:15.882 line:0%
(AUDIENCE LAUGHS)

00:00:15.982 --> 00:00:17.850 line:0%
When anyone is demanding an
apology,

00:00:18.317 --> 00:00:20.687 line:0%
unless they have hostages,
that's not news.

00:00:21.354 --> 00:00:23.590 line:0%
And when the offended group
are identified as

00:00:23.790 --> 00:00:26.526 line:0%
"the internet", "Twitter",
or "people"?

00:00:26.893 --> 00:00:29.095 line:0%
-It's nobody!
-(AUDIENCE LAUGHS)

00:00:29.428 --> 00:00:31.898 line:0%
I guarantee, when you click
on the story you find out

00:00:31.998 --> 00:00:34.366 line:0%
"the internet" is three losers
with a combined

00:00:34.467 --> 00:00:36.202 line:0%
Twitter following of their mom.

00:00:37.003 --> 00:00:39.639 line:0%
(APPLAUSE)

00:00:43.243 --> 00:00:45.545
I used to think
something was news

00:00:45.745 --> 00:00:47.613
if a journalist reported it.

00:00:47.713 --> 00:00:50.116
But really, I live in a world
where it's news

00:00:50.216 --> 00:00:53.519
if Mariah Carey's tit flops out,
because Twitter will respond

00:00:53.619 --> 00:00:56.689
and then a "journalist" reports
on the controversy.

00:00:57.357 --> 00:01:00.293
If a boob flops in the forest,
and no one is around to hear it,

00:01:00.493 --> 00:01:01.594
it doesn't make a sound.

00:01:01.694 --> 00:01:02.728
(AUDIENCE LAUGHS)

00:01:02.828 --> 00:01:05.465
But if three jackasses tweet
about it, it's news.

00:01:06.365 --> 00:01:08.634
Here's an example
from this week.

00:01:08.968 --> 00:01:11.437
This is a picture from the
premiere of<i> Red Sparrow,</i>

00:01:11.704 --> 00:01:12.972
which looks pretty good,
by the way.

00:01:13.072 --> 00:01:15.508
Jennifer Lawrence plays
a beautiful woman who uses

00:01:15.775 --> 00:01:18.144
sex as a weapon
on orders from Moscow.

00:01:18.244 --> 00:01:20.180
It's working title was
<i> I, Ivanka.</i>

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:22.148
(AUDIENCE LAUGHS)

00:01:22.248 --> 00:01:25.084
You were way ahead of me
on that one. (CHUCKLES)

00:01:25.752 --> 00:01:30.523 line:0%
Anyway, here's the headline from
<i>Elle</i> Online and 100 other sites.

00:01:30.623 --> 00:01:33.492 line:0%
"Jennifer Lawrence's Latest
<i> 'Red Sparrow'</i> Photocall

00:01:33.592 --> 00:01:36.295 line:0%
Has Twitter Calling Out
Gender Inequality"

00:01:37.029 --> 00:01:39.965 line:0%
Yes, see, see, 'cause the men
are wearing coats but she's not.

00:01:40.432 --> 00:01:42.234 line:0%
And even though that was her
choice,

00:01:42.735 --> 00:01:45.938 line:0%
someone with 11 followers
didn't like it, so the "story"

00:01:46.038 --> 00:01:48.474 line:0%
was reported
in<i> The New York Times,</i>

00:01:48.574 --> 00:01:52.411 line:0%
<i> The Washington Post,
 New York Post, Fox News,</i> BBC,

00:01:52.511 --> 00:01:55.715
<i> Vanity Fair, Chicago Tribune,
 The Guardian, National Review.</i>

00:01:56.382 --> 00:02:00.319
Now, all these esteemed news
organizations aren't saying

00:02:00.586 --> 00:02:02.155
they think it's a big deal,

00:02:02.355 --> 00:02:04.223
because they are serious
journalists.

00:02:04.323 --> 00:02:07.160
They'd rather be writing about
Syria and the oceans dying.

00:02:08.261 --> 00:02:09.695
But, oh, the humanity.

00:02:09.795 --> 00:02:11.764
Jennifer Lawrence
didn't have a coat.

00:02:11.864 --> 00:02:13.833
Wrap her up! Wrap her up!

00:02:14.266 --> 00:02:18.738
(LAUGHTER AND APPLAUSE)

00:02:20.439 --> 00:02:21.374
You know,

00:02:21.741 --> 00:02:25.378
this is not an outlier.
This is a constant and prominent

00:02:25.611 --> 00:02:27.647
part of today's journalism.

00:02:27.947 --> 00:02:31.083
Creating some bullshit non-issue
that a few trolls

00:02:31.183 --> 00:02:33.185
will predictably go apeshit over

00:02:33.452 --> 00:02:36.055
and then reporting on those
unrepresentative tweets,

00:02:36.155 --> 00:02:38.424
like all of America is talking
about nothing else.

00:02:39.225 --> 00:02:41.494
"Justin Timberlake used
a projection of Prince

00:02:41.594 --> 00:02:44.196
for his Super Bowl halftime show
and people are furious."

00:02:44.563 --> 00:02:48.333
-No. Nobody cared.
-(AUDIENCE LAUGHS)

00:02:49.134 --> 00:02:51.303
People are really mad that
Shaun White dragged

00:02:51.403 --> 00:02:53.406
the American flag
after he won the gold.

00:02:53.639 --> 00:02:56.142
No. Not even a little,
you fucking liars.

00:02:56.242 --> 00:02:59.745
(LAUGHTER AND APPLAUSE)

00:03:00.779 --> 00:03:03.949
"Weight Watchers is targeting
teens and Twitter is outraged."

00:03:04.049 --> 00:03:08.120
No, it isn't! It's the same
three people, and it's not hard

00:03:08.321 --> 00:03:10.523
to find three people who
are mad at anything.

00:03:10.623 --> 00:03:12.124
I could say, "Good morning"

00:03:12.224 --> 00:03:14.460
